WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.910
Scientists are driven to answer questions that, 

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.740
you know, they're confronted with in their everyday world. And the 

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.540
biologists in the 1800s were confronted with the fact that there were many 

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.040
different species on the face of the earth, and there were many differences between 

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:19.830
individuals,

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.080
even within a species.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.240
And so it was very natural,

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:24.550
I think,

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.470
to ask the question,

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.850
why aren't we all identical?

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.950
Why don't all humans look exactly alike?

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.150
Why don't humans look exactly like an ape?

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.220
What drives the differences?

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.390
That's the whole concept of inheritance, that somehow when a human has a kid,

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.070
it looks like a human and doesn't look like an ape.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:44.950
On the other hand,

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:45.520
the question is,

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:45.720
well,

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.150
once you accept that there is a system of inheritance which will allow things to be perpetuated,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.680
then how'd you ever get anything that was different from each other?

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.620
So if apes we think are the precursor to humans,

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.450
if you have a system which perpetuates apes, then how do you ever get a human out of an ape?

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.270
And so I think around them,

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:06.650
what the biologists were drawing was this paradox of there are clearly these ways for 

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.160
inheritance to work where it can perpetuate a species 

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.350
by having individuals,

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.360
the progeny, look like the parents,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:20.360
but there also had to be some way to change that inheritance to give rise to a new 

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:20.900
species.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:26.290
One idea is that there's some kind of genetic material that is transmitted from one organism to 

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:26.720
another,

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.870
which specifies that the progeny be exactly like their parents.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.950
Once you acknowledge that there's genetic material and that genetic 

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.400
change has to happen,

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.150
change to that genetic material, has to happen in order to generate new traits,

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.870
new species. There were two big models that tried to explain that. 

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:49.970
One of the models was championed by a scientist named Lamarck, who postulated 

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:53.040
that the change was in the genetic material that occurred,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.850
it was a consequence of the response to the environment,

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:56.820
that is,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.410
the environment somehow caused the genetic material to acquire a 

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:06.410
difference, a mutation you might call it, altered it's state such that it could now convey a new 

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:06.730
trait.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:07.730
I mean,

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:12.100
the example would be here, is that giraffes might have had short necks,

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:13.130
initially,

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.370
they could chew the leaves of the trees.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.290
And then what happened was the trees grew taller,

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:19.680
so the giraffes,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.430
in order to reach the leaves on trees,

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.430
stretch their necks,

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:28.110
and the process of stretching their necks caused them to change 

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.770
the genetic material within them,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.590
that gave rise then to baby giraffes,

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:33.630
which had longer necks.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.330
And so this was called the induced mutation model.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.080
The other model was championed by descendants of Darwin, 

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.260
and the basic idea there was that changes in genetic material 

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.330
happened all the time,

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:51.490
they happened spontaneously.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.410
And so in any population of giraffes,

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.410
most of them will be an average height of,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:57.810
let's say,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.720
whatever it is, 12 feet,

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:03.770
but there'll be an occasional giraffe which was 14 feet tall.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.110
And when the trees grow taller,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.810
the giraffe who happens to be 14 feet tall can reach those tall leaves,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:13.130
but the other giraffes that are 12 feet tall, can't. They die off.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.120
The giraffe that's 14 has babies and all its progeny does well,

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.730
and so now all of a sudden, all the giraffes are generally an average of 14 feet tall, instead of being 12 feet tall.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.370
And so this was called spontaneous mutation model.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.080
So those were the two models at the time. The controversy persisted for 50 

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.790
years because there were three main hurdles that really confronted solving or addressing 

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.330
which of the models was correct. 

